{"from": "site:bethany-al-eizariya", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-18-bethany-fifteen-stadia", "grade": "C", "note": "The excavated village of Bethany / al-Eizariya (Saller 1949-1953: houses, rock-cut tombs, wine-presses, cisterns; Persian-to-Roman pottery) confirms an ancient village near Jerusalem at the traditional location, reusing the evidence of Matthew 21/26 and Luke 24. Whether al-Eizariya occupies the ancient village is questioned (see the chapter complicates edges), so C."}
{"from": "observation:bethany-jerusalem-distance", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-18-bethany-fifteen-stadia", "grade": "C", "note": "Computed great-circle distances from the Wikipedia coordinates: 2882 m to the Temple Mount, 2433 m to the north-east wall of the Old City; fifteen stadia of 157.5-185 m (the article's stadion of 600 feet = 180 m gives 2700 m) equals 2363-2775 m. Plausibility of the distance only."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-ketubot-4-5", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-19-consolation-mourners", "grade": "C", "note": "'Even the poorest man of the Jewish people may not provide fewer than two flutes and a lamenting woman' - organized public mourning was customary and expected in Jewish funerals. Redacted c. 200 CE, so it may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly; it attests the funeral-mourning custom, not the consolation visits at the house specifically."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-ketubot-4-5", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-31-tomb-weeping", "grade": "C", "note": "The same passage: professional lamenting women were hired even for the poor, so weeping mourners around the bereaved family are the expected scene. Redacted c. 200 CE and may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly; the weeping at the tomb itself is not stated by the Mishnah."}
{"from": "text:4q521-2", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-24-resurrection-last-day", "grade": "C", "note": "A pre-Christian Qumran text expects God's agent to 'heal the badly wounded and make the dead live'. It attests the currency of resurrection hope in Judaism before the Gospel, not Martha's statement or its truth."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-sects", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-24-resurrection-last-day", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 18.1.3: the Pharisees hold that souls survive death with rewards and punishments under God's power - afterlife belief of the same party and period, though Josephus's formulation is not bodily resurrection 'at the last day'."}
{"from": "site:tombs-of-the-kings", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-38-cave-tomb-stone", "grade": "B", "note": "In this 1st-century CE rock-cut Jerusalem tomb (identified with Queen Helena of Adiabene's monument) 'the access... could be sealed closed by rolling a round stone across it, and the stone still remains in-situ' - the same closure practice in Jerusalem in the period, though not Lazarus's own tomb (reuse of the Matthew 27/28 evidence at its grade)."}
{"from": "site:tomb-of-lazarus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-38-cave-tomb-stone", "grade": "D", "note": "The cave shown at al-Eizariya has been identified as the tomb of the Gospel since at least the 4th century (Eusebius, Bordeaux pilgrim; Lazarium), but the Catholic Encyclopedia already calls the identification of this particular cave 'merely possible; it has no strong intrinsic or extrinsic authority' - a reception tradition, grade D. Archaeology confirms a 1st-century AD cemetery in the area."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-sects", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-47-council", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus names the Pharisees as one of the three real Jewish parties of the period ('the third sort of opinions was that of those called Pharisees'), alongside the chief-priestly aristocracy. It attests the groups, not this gathering."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-199-sanhedrin", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-47-council", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 20.9.1: in 62 CE Ananus 'assembled the sanhedrim of judges' in Jerusalem - the ruling council convening in the same city a generation later; it attests the institution, not this session."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-55-64", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-48-roman-fear", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus: under Pilate his soldiers killed Jews in Jerusalem during unrest over his standards and his aqueduct (Ant. 18.55-62) - Roman troops suppressing crowds in the holy city in the very governorship of the council's fear. It attests the condition, not that the fear was voiced or justified; it never attests the 70 CE destruction as a prediction. Regraded B to C by architect review: Pilate's documented violence makes the council's fear plausible (context); it does not attest the fear."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-2-varus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-48-roman-fear", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus (War 2.5.2): after the disturbances at Herod's death (4 BCE) Varus crucified about two thousand in Judaea - Roman collective reprisal against the nation a generation earlier; context, not the council's fear itself."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-caiaphas", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-49-caiaphas-that-year", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 18.35 and 18.95: 'Joseph Caiaphas' was made high priest by Valerius Gratus (c. 18 CE) and deprived of the high priesthood by Vitellius (36 CE) - the same man in the office at the time John's 'that year' marks (reuse of the Matthew 26 evidence at its grade)."}
{"from": "artifact:caiaphas-ossuary", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-49-caiaphas-that-year", "grade": "B", "note": "The ossuary twice inscribed 'Joseph, son of Caiaphas' (excavated 1990, Israel Museum) names the same high-priestly family in a first-century Jerusalem burial; the inscription carries no high-priestly title and the identification is challenged by some scholars, so B for the office (A on person:caiaphas)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-4-ephraim", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-54-ephraim", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus, War 4.9.1: Vespasian 'took Bethel and Ephraim, two small cities', garrisoning them on the way to Jerusalem - a city named Ephraim existed in the hill country near Bethel in the 1st century, the setting John's 'city called Ephraim' near the wilderness requires. Josephus does not tie it to Jesus."}
{"from": "text:eusebius-onomasticon-ephraim", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-54-ephraim", "grade": "D", "note": "Eusebius, Onomasticon, entry 458: 'Ephraim. \"Near the desert\" where the Christ (the Lord Jesus) came \"with his disciples\"', cross-referencing Ephron (418), 'a (very) large village (named) Ephraim twenty miles north of Jerusalem' - the 4th-century identification tradition; he also misassigns Ephron to Judah, and the identification is disputed, so D."}
{"from": "site:taybeh", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-54-ephraim", "grade": "D", "note": "The SWP (1882) suggested Taybeh (et-Taiyibeh) was ancient Ophrah of Benjamin, and prominent scholars have backed the identification since; the same village is the traditional site of John's Ephraim. A modern identification with disputed details, so D."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-pesachim-5-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-09-twelve-hours", "grade": "C", "note": "Mishnah Pesachim 5:1: the daily offering 'is slaughtered at eight and a half hours of the day... at nine and a half hours of the day' - daylight counted in hours from dawn, the same reckoning behind 'twelve hours of daylight'. Redacted c. 200 CE, so the practice may not be exactly that of the 30s CE."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-mikvaot-1-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-11-55-purify-before-passover", "grade": "C", "note": "Mishnah Mikvaot 1:1 grades immersion pools 'all are eligible... until they are lacking' - ritual immersion for purity was a standing institution. Redacted c. 200 CE and may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly; it attests the purification infrastructure, not the pre-Passover pilgrimage itself."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-caiaphas", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:jn-11-49-caiaphas-that-year", "note": "Josephus dates the tenure from appointment by Gratus (c. 18 CE) to removal by Vitellius (36 CE), about eighteen years - so John's 'high priest that year' cannot mean an annual appointment; it most likely marks the year of the council and of the approaching Passover, near the end of the tenure."}
{"from": "claim:jn-11-01-lazarus-of-bethany", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-10-38-martha-received", "note": "Luke 10:38-42 has Martha receiving Jesus in an unnamed village with her sister Mary; John 11:1 names the village Bethany and adds the brother Lazarus. Same household, different occasions."}
{"from": "claim:jn-11-02-mary-anointed", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-26-07-costly-ointment", "note": "John names the woman as Mary of Bethany and has her anoint his feet with her hair; Matthew's woman is unnamed and anoints his head. John defers the anointing itself to John 12:3."}
{"from": "claim:jn-11-08-stoning-attempt", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-10-31-stoning-attempt", "note": "John 11:8 recalls the stoning attempt the Jews made in John 10:31 (repeated at 10:39)."}
{"from": "claim:jn-11-37-opened-blind-eyes", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-09-01-blind-from-birth", "note": "A back-reference to John 9: the man whose eyes Jesus opened was blind from his birth."}
{"from": "claim:jn-11-38-cave-tomb-stone", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-27-59-60-new-rock-tomb", "note": "Lazarus's tomb is a cave with a stone lying against it (John 11:38); Jesus's tomb is a new rock-cut tomb closed by a great rolled stone (Matthew 27:60). Same burial practice, different tombs."}
{"from": "claim:jn-11-38-cave-tomb-stone", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-28-02-06-tomb-stone", "note": "The stone-across-the-entrance detail in both tombs; Matthew 28:2's stone is rolled away, John 11:39's is taken away."}
{"from": "claim:jn-11-49-caiaphas-that-year", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-26-03-caiaphas-high-priest", "note": "Same man and office; John adds the twice-repeated phrase 'high priest that year', which Matthew lacks."}
{"from": "text:daniel-12-2", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:bible-api-daniel-12-2"}
{"from": "claim:jn-11-24-resurrection-last-day", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:daniel-12-2", "note": "Cross-reference: 'many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake' (Daniel 12:2) is the classic scriptural basis of the resurrection hope Martha voices. Biblical cross-reference, not independent evidence."}
{"from": "claim:jn-11-55-purify-before-passover", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-02-13-passover-pilgrimage", "note": "The same going-up-to-Jerusalem-for-Passover custom as John 2:13; John 11:55 adds the purification before the feast."}
{"from": "passage:jn-11-02", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-26-07", "note": "The Bethany anointing: John 11:2 anticipates the anointing told in John 12:3 and Matthew 26:6-13 / Mark 14:3; Matthew's woman is unnamed and anoints his head."}
{"from": "passage:jn-11-02", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mk-14-03", "note": "The Bethany anointing; see the Matthew 26:7 note."}
{"from": "passage:jn-11-47", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-26-03", "note": "The leaders gather to deal with Jesus: John after the raising of Lazarus, before the final week; Matthew two days before the Passover, in Caiaphas's court."}
{"from": "passage:jn-11-53", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-26-04", "note": "The council resolves to kill Jesus: 'from that day they took counsel that they might put him to death' parallels Matthew 26:4's plot to take and kill him."}
